WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:05.100
TikTok bills itself as “the last&nbsp;
sunny corner of the internet”

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:07.680
But many people see a dark side.

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:12.900
Beneath the app’s surface lies&nbsp;
incredibly advanced artificial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:16.920
intelligence that has learned what&nbsp;
people like with stunning impact.

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:21.780
TikTok signed up 1 billion users&nbsp;
in half the time it took Facebook.

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:22.180
Facebook launched in 2004 and hit 1 billion users&nbsp;
in 2012, so took 8 years). TikTok, launched in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:22.560
2016, reached the milestone in just five years,&nbsp;
in 2021 - can we show this on-screen simply?

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:27.720
The West has always accused China of&nbsp;
copying technological advances, but now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:31.020
Silicon Valley is rushing&nbsp;
to mimic the Chinese app.

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:34.440
In response to TikTok’s&nbsp;
short video format, YouTube&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:37.800
introduced Shorts. And Instagram released Reels.

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:43.140
Unlike Instagram, where your feed consists&nbsp;
of photos and videos from people you follow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:47.280
TikTok relies on a strategy&nbsp;
of recommendations first.

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:52.320
You find yourself watching videos from people&nbsp;
you never chose to watch, who you don’t follow.

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:55.800
It’s what TikTok believes will most appeal to you.

00:00:55.800 --> 00:01:02.520
Imagine the effect of binging on an endless&nbsp;
stream of 15 or 30-second videos…for an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:07.200
hour and a half a day - the average&nbsp;
amount of time users spend on TikTok.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:09.660
That dopamine rush is addictive.

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:14.520
And, a study from the Technical University&nbsp;
of Denmark found that the constant stream&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:19.260
of information from social media can&nbsp;
shorten our attention span over time.

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:24.360
“...and that this effect occurs - not&nbsp;
only on social media - but also across&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:29.460
diverse domains including books, web&nbsp;
searches, movie popularity, and more.”

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:34.800
In other words, a shorter attention&nbsp;
span impacts every aspect of your life.

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:38.340
When was the last time you sat&nbsp;
through an entire movie at home?

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:42.300
Can you even bear to watch older&nbsp;
movies that are slower paced?

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:44.340
No Mr. Matuschek, it’s not for us.

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:50.040
Or worse: what happens when our society is&nbsp;
filled with people who lack the focus to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:53.460
think things through over long periods or&nbsp;
complete projects that take a lot of time?

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:58.860
In the way that TV news in the seventies&nbsp;
shortened reports to 1 minute 30 seconds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:01.260
to keep the attention of the television audience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:05.760
TikTok is now shortening our attention&nbsp;
span from minutes to seconds.

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:11.340
Anything longer isn’t worth our time, unless we’re&nbsp;
really, really interested in the subject matter.

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:16.620
Does a 30-minute YouTube video now feel&nbsp;
like an unbearably long documentary?

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:21.300
Our addiction to the screen is&nbsp;
literally damaging our brains.

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:25.320
Multiple studies have shown that&nbsp;
those addicted to gaming and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:28.800
internet have a reduced volume&nbsp;
of gray matter in their brain.

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:32.460
Gray matter has a large number of neurons present,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:35.700
which allows it to process&nbsp;
information in the brain.

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:41.040
So, spending too much time on your phone is&nbsp;
literally causing your brain tissue to atrophy.

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:45.000
Much of the damage occurs in&nbsp;
the frontal lobe - the area&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:50.760
that largely determines success in life -&nbsp;
be it academics, career, or relationships.

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:54.840
Then there’s the issue of the quality&nbsp;
of the videos you’re watching.

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:58.920
There’s been a lot of talk lately&nbsp;
that TikTok in the US is different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:03.000
from the version shown in China,&nbsp;
where TikTok is known as Douyin.

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:08.400
A user wrote on Quora that his TikTok feed “is&nbsp;
filled with teenagers doing stupid things.”

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:13.680
Whereas his Douyin feed is “filled with&nbsp;
self-improvement, photography, travel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:20.160
calligraphy, life hacks, trivia, interesting new&nbsp;
products, and of course, cute dog/cat videos.”

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:25.680
By the way, in China, those aged 14&nbsp;
and under are limited to using Douyin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:31.140
for 40 minutes a day and are blocked&nbsp;
from using it between 10 pm to 6 am.

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:34.200
The differences between the two TikToks is stark,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:37.620
as social media expert Tristan&nbsp;
Harris told 60 Minutes.

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:41.400
“...they make their domestic&nbsp;
version a spinach version of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:44.640
TikTok, while they ship the opium&nbsp;
version to the rest of the world.”&nbsp;

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:50.220
Perhaps that speaks to the difference in career&nbsp;
aspirations of Chinese and American children.

00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:54.120
In China, most kids dream&nbsp;
of becoming an astronaut.

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:58.380
In America, most kids aspire&nbsp;
to be social media influencers.

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:05.220
Toward the end of Donald Trump’s presidency, he&nbsp;
tried and failed to ban TikTok in the U.S. or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:11.160
force the Beijing company that owns it, ByteDance,&nbsp;
to sell TikTok’s American arm to a domestic owner.

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:15.780
The most frequent criticism&nbsp;
of TikTok is privacy issues.

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:21.240
China can demand whatever information it&nbsp;
likes from companies based in its country.

00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:26.640
A damning report from BuzzFeed&nbsp;
in June 2022 claimed “U.S. user&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:30.120
data was repeatedly accessed&nbsp;
from (TikTok staff in) China”

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:35.760
TikTok has always insisted that information&nbsp;
from the U.S. is stored in the U.S,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:39.960
which right now is on the servers&nbsp;
of American company Oracle.

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:45.840
And at a recent New York Times event, its CEO&nbsp;
denied handing over Americans’ data to China.

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:49.860
No foreign government has&nbsp;
asked us for U.S. data before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:53.280
really they haven’t, and if&nbsp;
they did, we would say no.

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:56.280
But Washington isn’t buying it. In December,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.280 --> 00:05:00.660
lawmakers in the House and Senate&nbsp;
introduced legislation that would ban&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:06.360
TikTok in the U.S. after the FBI warned&nbsp;
it could be used by China for spying.

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:12.120
The Senate has voted to ban TikTok from all&nbsp;
government-issued phones. As of this video,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.713 --> 00:05:18.873
TikTok is growing exponentially.

00:05:18.873 --> 00:05:24.273
It was the most downloaded app in&nbsp;
2021 and remains on top in 2022.

00:05:24.273 --> 00:05:27.213
Although it may have started off as a dancing app,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.213 --> 00:05:30.693
it is increasingly becoming&nbsp;
a news source for Gen Z.

00:05:32.013 --> 00:05:36.093
Like during Johnny Depp’s defamation&nbsp;
trial against his ex-wife Amber Heard.

00:05:36.093 --> 00:05:37.533
My dog stepped on a bee

00:05:37.533 --> 00:05:40.233
Newsthink does have a TikTok account,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.233 --> 00:05:44.193
where I hope to educate and enlighten&nbsp;
people…in a short span of time.

00:05:44.193 --> 00:05:48.393
But…it’s exactly that short&nbsp;
span of time that’s problematic.

00:05:48.993 --> 00:05:54.093
We constantly crave more and more&nbsp;
information in less and less time.

00:05:54.093 --> 00:05:56.973
And THAT may one day, spell disaster.

00:05:58.353 --> 00:06:01.113
TikTok is slowly becoming a news source.

00:06:01.113 --> 00:06:03.831
There’s a lot of news these&nbsp;
days about climate change.

00:06:03.831 --> 00:06:04.713
It will take a lot to end the crisis. And one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.713 --> 00:06:07.773
company is trying to tackle&nbsp;
it simply and effectively.

00:06:07.773 --> 00:06:13.113
Wren is a website and app that makes it easy&nbsp;
for you to calculate your carbon footprint.

00:06:13.113 --> 00:06:15.693
By answering a few questions about your lifestyle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.693 --> 00:06:18.873
you can discover your carbon&nbsp;
footprint and how to reduce it.

00:06:18.873 --> 00:06:23.793
You can make a monthly contribution. This will go&nbsp;
toward projects like this one which provides clean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.793 --> 00:06:29.073
cooking fuel to refugees in Uganda. You’ll receive&nbsp;
monthly updates on the projects you support.

00:06:29.073 --> 00:06:35.253
Since Wrench launched in 2019, they’ve&nbsp;
sent over $1 million in funding to projects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.253 --> 00:06:39.993
planting trees, protecting rainforests,&nbsp;
and otherwise fighting the climate crisis.

00:06:39.993 --> 00:06:43.593
You can offset your carbon&nbsp;
footprint today on Wren by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.593 --> 00:06:45.933
signing up with the custom link in my description.

00:06:45.933 --> 00:06:52.413
The first 100 people who sign up will&nbsp;
have 10 extra trees planted in their name.

00:06:52.413 --> 00:06:56.073
Thanks for watching. For Newsthink, I'm Cindy Pom.

